WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.207
Welcome to Module 1 of the
Microsoft Automation learning path:

2
00:00:04.207 --> 00:00:06.125
Graph and Azure foundations.

3
00:00:06.125 --> 00:00:11.384
The books keep asking for evidence a script
collects: Conditional Access exported

4
00:00:11.384 --> 00:00:16.262
as code, device compliance read back,
control evidence gathered on a schedule.

5
00:00:16.262 --> 00:00:18.887
Almost all of it goes
through Microsoft Graph.

6
00:00:18.887 --> 00:00:24.003
This module covers what every such script
has to get right before it does anything

7
00:00:24.003 --> 00:00:27.689
useful: which cloud it calls,
how it signs in without a secret,

8
00:00:27.689 --> 00:00:31.225
which permissions it asks for,
and four request patterns.

9
00:00:31.225 --> 00:00:36.087
Then you'll see the example client prove
each rule on a laptop, with no tenant.

10
00:00:36.087 --> 00:00:41.390
Modules 2 and 3 apply all of
this to Entra ID and Intune.

11
00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:46.178
Why start here?

12
00:00:46.178 --> 00:00:48.302
Picture your first evidence collector.

13
00:00:48.302 --> 00:00:50.414
It works in a commercial test tenant.

14
00:00:50.414 --> 00:00:53.747
Then, the day it points
at a GCC High tenant,

15
00:00:53.747 --> 00:00:57.765
it fails, or worse, sends
its token to the wrong host.

16
00:00:57.765 --> 00:01:02.078
It reads the first page of users and
reports a tenant of a hundred people,

17
00:01:02.078 --> 00:01:05.440
and nobody notices because
the number looks plausible.

18
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.643
It signs in with a client
secret that nobody rotates,

19
00:01:08.643 --> 00:01:12.665
though ScubaGear says application
passwords should be blocked.

20
00:01:12.665 --> 00:01:17.365
And it asks for broad permissions, so a
compromised job could do more than read.

21
00:01:17.365 --> 00:01:21.740
Each of those has a fix, and each
fix is a unit in this module.

22
00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:25.813
The cost of skipping them is simple:
a collector that doesn't page,

23
00:01:25.813 --> 00:01:30.940
retry and keep delta links produces
evidence that looks complete, and isn't.

24
00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:37.428
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of the module,

25
00:01:37.428 --> 00:01:40.102
which takes about eighty-five
minutes with the lab.

26
00:01:40.102 --> 00:01:44.934
You'll choose the right sign-in
host, Graph host and PowerShell

27
00:01:44.934 --> 00:01:49.291
environment for a commercial,
GCC, GCC High or DoD tenant.

28
00:01:49.291 --> 00:01:51.760
You'll pick a credential
for a scheduled script

29
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.040
and know who can consent
to its permissions.

30
00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.061
You'll find the least-privileged
permission for a call,

31
00:01:57.061 --> 00:01:59.277
and know what a narrow permission hides.

32
00:01:59.277 --> 00:02:01.677
You'll describe the four request patterns.

33
00:02:01.677 --> 00:02:04.341
And you'll run the example
client's checks yourself.

34
00:02:04.341 --> 00:02:07.740
For the lab you need Python
and PowerShell on a laptop.

35
00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.320
You don't need a tenant until Lab 2.

36
00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:18.699
Microsoft Graph and Entra ID run as
separate national cloud deployments,

37
00:02:18.699 --> 00:02:22.720
and a script has to call the hosts
of the cloud its tenant lives in.

38
00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.745
Commercial and Microsoft 365 GCC
tenants use the same worldwide hosts:

39
00:02:27.745 --> 00:02:32.685
login dot microsoftonline dot com
for sign-in and graph dot microsoft

40
00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:37.370
dot com for Graph, with the Global
environment in Graph PowerShell.

41
00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:40.445
Microsoft's own words are
that a GCC environment

42
00:02:40.445 --> 00:02:43.520
should continue using
the worldwide endpoints.

43
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:49.272
GCC High and DoD both sign in at
login dot microsoftonline dot U S.

44
00:02:49.272 --> 00:02:53.721
GCC High's Graph host is
graph dot microsoft dot U S,

45
00:02:53.721 --> 00:02:57.845
and DoD's is dod dash graph
dot microsoft dot U S.

46
00:02:57.845 --> 00:03:02.615
In Graph PowerShell those are the U
S Gov and U S Gov DoD environments,

47
00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:07.840
and Azure PowerShell and the Azure
CLI switch to Azure U S Government.

48
00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.103
Beyond the hosts, five things change.

49
00:03:14.103 --> 00:03:18.454
The token's scope names the
cloud, so app-only tokens for GCC

50
00:03:18.454 --> 00:03:22.715
High ask for graph dot microsoft
dot U S slash dot default.

51
00:03:22.715 --> 00:03:26.097
The Python azure-identity
credential signs in to the public

52
00:03:26.097 --> 00:03:29.277
cloud unless you give it the
Azure Government authority.

53
00:03:29.277 --> 00:03:32.785
Apps are registered per
cloud: an app registered in a

54
00:03:32.785 --> 00:03:36.291
commercial tenant can't read
a GCC High tenant's data.

55
00:03:36.291 --> 00:03:41.553
Graph Explorer isn't supported on the GCC
High, DoD or China endpoints, so a query

56
00:03:41.553 --> 00:03:46.978
you proved in Graph Explorer has to be
retested against the government endpoint.

57
00:03:46.978 --> 00:03:48.516
That's design guidance.

58
00:03:48.516 --> 00:03:52.920
And every Graph API reference
page has a national cloud table;

59
00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:56.203
check it before you rely
on an API in a new cloud.

60
00:03:56.203 --> 00:04:00.490
GCC needs none of this, but it can
lack features, which is the subject of

61
00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:05.580
the Federal Modernization Workbook's
Cloud Boundary and Landing Zone book.

62
00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:14.599
A scheduled script signs in as itself, not
as a person: an app registration and its

63
00:04:14.599 --> 00:04:20.268
service principal, with application
permissions, using the client credentials flow.

64
00:04:20.268 --> 00:04:23.206
What matters is what the app
proves its identity with.

65
00:04:23.206 --> 00:04:27.107
In order of preference, which is
design guidance from the sources:

66
00:04:27.107 --> 00:04:31.009
a managed identity when the script
runs on an Azure resource or an

67
00:04:31.009 --> 00:04:35.055
Arc-enabled server, because there's
no credential to store or rotate.

68
00:04:35.055 --> 00:04:38.356
Workload identity federation
for GitHub Actions,

69
00:04:38.356 --> 00:04:41.893
Kubernetes, or any platform
that issues its own tokens.

70
00:04:41.893 --> 00:04:45.709
A certificate issued by a
certificate authority anywhere else,

71
00:04:45.709 --> 00:04:48.230
which Microsoft recommends over a secret.

72
00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:50.485
And a client secret, which you avoid:

73
00:04:50.485 --> 00:04:54.217
ScubaGear says application
passwords should be blocked.

74
00:04:54.217 --> 00:04:55.393
Then consent.

75
00:04:55.393 --> 00:04:59.180
Graph application permissions need
a Privileged Role Administrator.

76
00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:01.809
Plan that approval step:
it's the moment someone

77
00:05:01.809 --> 00:05:04.880
with privilege decides
what the script may read.

78
00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:10.692
Graph has two kinds of permission.

79
00:05:10.692 --> 00:05:13.237
Delegated permissions act for a signed-in

80
00:05:13.237 --> 00:05:15.854
user and are limited by
what that user can do.

81
00:05:15.854 --> 00:05:19.967
Application permissions act as
the app, across the whole tenant.

82
00:05:19.967 --> 00:05:23.327
Every API reference page
lists permissions in order,

83
00:05:23.327 --> 00:05:27.680
least privileged first, and you ask
for the least one that does the job.

84
00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:31.214
In Graph PowerShell, Find
dash Mg Graph Command and

85
00:05:31.214 --> 00:05:34.580
Find dash Mg Graph Permission
help you find them.

86
00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:37.390
Where a narrower
permission exists, use it:

87
00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:42.208
enabling and disabling accounts
needs User dot Enable Disable Account

88
00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:46.142
dot All plus User dot Read dot
All, not read-write on all users.

89
00:05:46.142 --> 00:05:48.442
And watch what a narrow permission hides.

90
00:05:48.442 --> 00:05:53.354
Objects you can't fully read still
come back, with only an ID and a type.

91
00:05:53.354 --> 00:05:56.555
A null property may mean
not permitted, not empty.

92
00:05:56.555 --> 00:06:00.724
The example kit keeps every call
and its permission in one file,

93
00:06:00.724 --> 00:06:05.660
graph-calls dot JSON, and a task can
make only the calls listed for it.

94
00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:10.810
Use the current tools.

95
00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:16.833
The Microsoft Graph PowerShell S D K, for
interactive and scheduled administration,

96
00:06:16.833 --> 00:06:20.447
with Invoke dash Mg Graph
Request for any endpoint.

97
00:06:20.447 --> 00:06:24.592
The Microsoft Graph Python S D
K for services and pipelines,

98
00:06:24.592 --> 00:06:27.636
where a page iterator
follows pages for you.

99
00:06:27.636 --> 00:06:30.680
azure-identity in Python,
for managed identity,

100
00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:33.335
certificate and federated credentials.

101
00:06:33.335 --> 00:06:39.197
Az PowerShell and the Azure CLI for Azure
Resource Manager, which Module 4 covers.

102
00:06:39.197 --> 00:06:43.335
And not AzureAD, AzureAD
Preview or MSOnline.

103
00:06:43.335 --> 00:06:49.873
Microsoft deprecated them on March 30,
2024, said they'd work only until March 30,

104
00:06:49.873 --> 00:06:53.997
2025, and recommends
Microsoft Graph PowerShell.

105
00:06:53.997 --> 00:06:59.035
The example client calls the REST API
directly with the standard library.

106
00:06:59.035 --> 00:07:01.772
That isn't advice against the S D Ks.

107
00:07:01.772 --> 00:07:07.480
It keeps every request visible, so you can
read and test each pattern line by line.

108
00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:14.317
Every Graph script needs
the same four patterns.

109
00:07:14.317 --> 00:07:15.543
One, paging.

110
00:07:15.543 --> 00:07:20.257
Collections come back a page at a time:
a hundred users by default, up to nine

111
00:07:20.257 --> 00:07:25.204
hundred ninety-nine with top, or five
hundred when you select sign-in activity.

112
00:07:25.204 --> 00:07:29.105
Follow the next link, unchanged,
until it stops appearing.

113
00:07:29.105 --> 00:07:30.393
Two, throttling.

114
00:07:30.393 --> 00:07:35.155
Over a limit, Graph answers four
twenty-nine with a Retry-After header.

115
00:07:35.155 --> 00:07:39.180
Wait that long, because retrying at
once keeps counting against you.

116
00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:40.530
Three, batching.

117
00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:45.550
A batch takes at most twenty requests, and
each one is still throttled on its own,

118
00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:49.180
so a batch can succeed while
some requests inside it fail.

119
00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:50.767
Check every status.

120
00:07:50.767 --> 00:07:51.930
Four, delta.

121
00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.754
Follow the next links
until you get a delta link,

122
00:07:54.754 --> 00:07:57.505
store it, and next time
you get only the changes.

123
00:07:57.505 --> 00:08:01.306
And some advanced Entra queries,
like count and search, need the

124
00:08:01.306 --> 00:08:06.490
ConsistencyLevel eventual header, or
they fail or return something different.

125
00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:13.503
The limits are layered, and the
first one you reach applies.

126
00:08:13.503 --> 00:08:16.652
Every service allows a
hundred thirty thousand

127
00:08:16.652 --> 00:08:20.039
requests per ten seconds
per app, across all tenants.

128
00:08:20.039 --> 00:08:23.636
Entra ID objects, like users,
groups and applications,

129
00:08:23.636 --> 00:08:28.784
use cost-based resource units instead:
thirty-five hundred, five thousand or

130
00:08:28.784 --> 00:08:34.177
eight thousand per ten seconds per app and
tenant, depending on the tenant's size.

131
00:08:34.177 --> 00:08:35.778
Writes have their own limits.

132
00:08:35.778 --> 00:08:38.552
A request's cost changes
with what you ask for.

133
00:08:38.552 --> 00:08:41.857
Select lowers it by one,
expand raises it by one,

134
00:08:41.857 --> 00:08:44.553
and a top under twenty lowers it by one.

135
00:08:44.553 --> 00:08:50.065
Listing a group's transitive members costs
five, against three for direct members.

136
00:08:50.065 --> 00:08:54.965
And a background job, like an evidence
collector, can send a low throttle priority.

137
00:08:54.965 --> 00:08:59.930
Microsoft recommends high priority
only for requests a user started.

138
00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:04.405
Try it.

139
00:09:04.405 --> 00:09:10.155
Lab 1 runs on a laptop in the examples
folder, against fixtures, with no tenant.

140
00:09:10.155 --> 00:09:14.341
Ask for a cloud name the client
doesn't know, G C C High with capitals,

141
00:09:14.341 --> 00:09:18.217
and you get a value error that
lists the four names it accepts.

142
00:09:18.217 --> 00:09:21.256
An unknown name is an
error, never a default.

143
00:09:21.256 --> 00:09:26.396
List the permissions the stale-users task
needs, and you get exactly two: AuditLog

144
00:09:26.396 --> 00:09:31.230
dot Read dot All and User dot Read dot
All, computed from the calls it declares.

145
00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:35.433
Make a call the Conditional Access
task doesn't declare, and it refuses,

146
00:09:35.433 --> 00:09:39.418
telling you to add it to graph-calls
dot JSON with its permission.

147
00:09:39.418 --> 00:09:45.155
Send a GCC High token to the worldwide
Graph host, and it refuses again.

148
00:09:45.155 --> 00:09:47.042
Nothing was sent in either case.

149
00:09:47.042 --> 00:09:50.380
Then nine tests prove
paging, retries, batching,

150
00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:54.743
delta and the advanced-query
header against a fake transport.

151
00:09:54.743 --> 00:09:59.101
The lab repeats the rules in
PowerShell, and the full suites pass:

152
00:09:59.101 --> 00:10:04.480
ninety-four in pytest and fifty-six in
Pester, counting Module 4's Arc tests.

153
00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:12.274
Lab 2 moves to your own test
tenant, worldwide or GCC,

154
00:10:12.274 --> 00:10:15.355
never production, with fictional data.

155
00:10:15.355 --> 00:10:20.493
You register one app in the tenant's own
cloud, with a certificate and no secret.

156
00:10:20.493 --> 00:10:24.270
You grant it one application
permission, Policy dot Read dot All,

157
00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:27.692
with admin consent from a
Privileged Role Administrator.

158
00:10:27.692 --> 00:10:31.279
You run the Conditional Access
export, which only reads.

159
00:10:31.279 --> 00:10:35.724
The developer workbook's Chapter 02
then has you see the token check work,

160
00:10:35.724 --> 00:10:40.317
by adding a permission the task doesn't
need, and the wrong cloud fail closed.

161
00:10:40.317 --> 00:10:44.739
And you back it out: remove the permission,
delete the app registration and its

162
00:10:44.739 --> 00:10:49.368
certificate, and delete the output folder,
because it holds the tenant's policies.

163
00:10:49.368 --> 00:10:54.610
The authors haven't run Lab 2; it follows
the workbook chapter step by step.

164
00:10:58.360 --> 00:10:59.672
Where are agencies?

165
00:10:59.672 --> 00:11:05.710
Most on this path run a Microsoft
365 GCC tenant with commercial Azure.

166
00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:10.124
Their scripts are often older than
the current tools: AzureAD cmdlets,

167
00:11:10.124 --> 00:11:14.385
a client secret in a scheduled task,
a host name written into the code.

168
00:11:14.385 --> 00:11:16.398
Some run GCC High.

169
00:11:16.398 --> 00:11:21.376
For what this module relies on: in GCC
High and DoD, the app is registered

170
00:11:21.376 --> 00:11:26.097
in the government cloud itself,
and Graph Explorer isn't supported.

171
00:11:26.097 --> 00:11:30.586
Microsoft's Azure Government
roadmap lists workload identities as

172
00:11:30.586 --> 00:11:35.311
generally available at FedRAMP High
and DoD impact levels four and five.

173
00:11:35.311 --> 00:11:39.847
And when a managed identity is an app's
federated credential, the audience

174
00:11:39.847 --> 00:11:44.385
value differs: the U S Gov token exchange
audience in the government cloud.

175
00:11:44.385 --> 00:11:49.216
The interim paths: rewrite retired-module
scripts on Graph PowerShell,

176
00:11:49.216 --> 00:11:52.983
use a certificate on premises
until the server is on Arc,

177
00:11:52.983 --> 00:11:56.790
and treat a GCC High move
as a configuration change.

178
00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:03.195
This module's subject is
the collector itself,

179
00:12:03.195 --> 00:12:07.178
so its requirements are the ones the
collector's identity has to meet.

180
00:12:07.178 --> 00:12:12.171
Least privilege, A C six in NIST special
publication eight hundred fifty-three:

181
00:12:12.171 --> 00:12:15.628
each script holds only the
permissions its calls need.

182
00:12:15.628 --> 00:12:19.186
Authenticator management, I
A five: no long-lived secret,

183
00:12:19.186 --> 00:12:24.602
and ScubaGear's application-credential
policies apply to the program's own apps too.

184
00:12:24.602 --> 00:12:26.718
Continuous monitoring, C A seven,

185
00:12:26.718 --> 00:12:30.165
depends on collectors that
return complete results.

186
00:12:30.165 --> 00:12:34.394
And BOD 25-01 requires
the SCuBA assessment tools

187
00:12:34.394 --> 00:12:37.565
and continuous reporting
for in-scope tenants.

188
00:12:37.565 --> 00:12:41.490
Appendix A of the Identity and
Directory book has the mapping.

189
00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:46.284
The evidence an ISSO keeps: for each
collector, its cloud, endpoint,

190
00:12:46.284 --> 00:12:51.700
app registration, credential type,
exact permissions and consent record,

191
00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:55.700
plus the call list and its
tests in the repository.

192
00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:02.950
Check yourself with two questions
from the module's knowledge check.

193
00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:07.850
First: your tenant is Microsoft 365 GCC.

194
00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:09.988
Which Graph host does your collector call?

195
00:13:09.988 --> 00:13:13.338
The answer is b, graph
dot microsoft dot com,

196
00:13:13.338 --> 00:13:16.688
because GCC uses the worldwide endpoints.

197
00:13:16.688 --> 00:13:19.097
Second: a batch returns two hundred,

198
00:13:19.097 --> 00:13:23.000
but two of its twenty requests
returned four twenty-nine.

199
00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:24.350
What should the client do?

200
00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:25.562
The answer is c.

201
00:13:25.562 --> 00:13:28.390
Each request in a batch
is throttled on its own,

202
00:13:28.390 --> 00:13:33.225
so check every response's status and
retry the throttled ones after their wait.

203
00:13:33.225 --> 00:13:38.510
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

204
00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:43.085
To sum up.

205
00:13:43.085 --> 00:13:48.254
You matched each cloud to its hosts, its
scope and its PowerShell environment,

206
00:13:48.254 --> 00:13:51.323
and learned that GCC is
the worldwide service.

207
00:13:51.323 --> 00:13:55.455
You put the credentials in order,
managed identity, federation,

208
00:13:55.455 --> 00:14:00.223
certificate, never a secret, and found
who consents to Graph permissions.

209
00:14:00.223 --> 00:14:04.235
You found least-privileged permissions
and what a narrow one hides.

210
00:14:04.235 --> 00:14:06.360
You learned the four request patterns.

211
00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:10.004
And in the lab you saw the
client refuse an unknown cloud,

212
00:14:10.004 --> 00:14:12.460
an undeclared call and the wrong host.

213
00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:14.181
Before you ship a collector,

214
00:14:14.181 --> 00:14:17.923
work through the developer
workbook's Chapter 02 checklist.

215
00:14:17.923 --> 00:14:24.101
Next is Module 2, Entra ID through
Microsoft Graph: users, groups, Conditional

216
00:14:24.101 --> 00:14:30.740
Access, PIM, applications and logs, with
five read-only checks mapped to ScubaGear.
